{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"armless-abased-sheldrake","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Problem\n\n`rebar-autodeploy-errors` has been in ALARM continuously since 2026-09-20T11:01Z,\nemitting an `AUTODEPLOY_ERROR` marker on every `rebar-autodeploy.timer` tick. The\nalarm condition is FALSE: MCP is healthy, production is at `main` tip, and the\nreview gate is fully functional. The marker is produced by a defect in the MCP\nlive-backend self-heal detector, which selects an EXITED container from an older\ndeploy as the \"live\" backend.\n\nThis is not cosmetic. A permanently-firing `mcp-live-backend-down` marker means a\nREAL MCP backend outage is now invisible -- the alarm can no longer distinguish\nthe two, so the signal it exists to carry has been destroyed.\n\n## Verbatim evidence\n\nStill firing at the time of writing:\n\n    2026-09-20T13:19:34+0000 autodeploy.sh[865945]: AUTODEPLOY_ERROR {\"ts\": 1789910374,\n      \"reason\": \"mcp-live-backend-down\",\n      \"detail\": \"live mcp backend rebar-mcp-c0b1ad2e8e92-8092 on port 8092 is NOT running;\n       /mcp is failing. Retained (never reaped: nginx still points here);\n       awaiting restart/redeploy\"}\n    2026-09-20T13:19:34+0000 autodeploy.sh[865945]: {\"event\":\"autodeploy\",\"ts\":1789910374,\n      \"msg\":\"up to date (e84e4c5e9439bcfd27875d152de36eb40eb4f46b); no-op\"}\n\nNote the pairing: the SAME tick reports `up to date ... no-op` while also claiming\nthe live backend is down. Those two cannot both be true, which is the tell.\n\nOnset, showing the detector firing one second before the redeploy it demands:\n\n    11:01:20Z msg: \"mcp cutover complete: /mcp upstream now 127.0.0.1:8092 (deploy DONE...)\"\n    11:01:20Z msg: \"mcp retire: 'docker stop --time 1260' rebar-mcp-feca8a211eea-8093 ...\"\n    11:01:20Z AUTODEPLOY_ERROR {\"reason\": \"mcp-live-backend-down\",\n                \"detail\": \"live mcp backend rebar-mcp-c0b1ad2e8e92-8092 ... is NOT running\"}\n    11:01:21Z msg: \"mcp redeployed + healthy at 127.0.0.1:8092\"\n\n## Root cause\n\nLatent bug introduced by Gerrit change **2825**, commit\n`7f014af789038785b9d79dc713bf1d72b20e5747` (\"Restart dead MCP live backend\"), which\nadded `mcp_live_backend_name()` / `mcp_self_heal_live_backend()` to\n`infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh` together with a `HostConfig.PortBindings` fallback so\nthat an EXITED container can still be mapped to a port.\n\nTriggered by an ordinary port ping-pong across three consecutive deploys:\n\n1. change 3004 (`c0b1ad2e8e9`) ran MCP on port **8092**.\n2. change 3006 (`feca8a211ee`) cut MCP over to **8093** and stopped the 8092 container,\n   leaving `rebar-mcp-c0b1ad2e8e92-8092` in state `exited`.\n3. change 3007 (`e84e4c5e943`) cut MCP back to **8092**, creating the real live\n   container `rebar-mcp-e84e4c5e9439-8092`.\n\nNow TWO containers resolve to port 8092 -- the exited `c0b1ad2e8e92` one via the\n`HostConfig.PortBindings` fallback, and the genuine running `e84e4c5e9439` one. The\ndetector selects the exited container, reports it as the live backend, finds it not\nrunning, and emits the marker. Because it also RETAINS it (\"never reaped: nginx still\npoints here\"), the stale container is never cleaned up and the loop is self-perpetuating.\n\nThe `PortBindings` fallback is correct in isolation -- its purpose is to identify a\nlive backend that has died. The defect is that it is consulted even when a RUNNING\nmanaged container already owns the live port, so a historical port binding outranks\npresent reality.\n\n## Marker rate\n\n67 markers from 11:01Z to 13:19Z, bucketed per 15 minutes: 7, 8, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 3.\nThat is ~one marker per `rebar-autodeploy.timer` tick (~2 min), NOT one per deploy\nbackoff. This explains the observed CloudWatch rate of 5-9 per 15-minute period, which\nwas otherwise inconsistent with the alarm's `BACKOFF_CAP=900s` design assumption.\n\n## What is NOT wrong (measured, so the fix stays scoped)\n\n- Production is NOT behind. `/var/lib/rebar/deployed-sha`, `bot-deployed-sha` and\n  `mcp-deployed-sha` all read `e84e4c5e9439bcfd27875d152de36eb40eb4f46b`, equal to\n  `origin/main`; behind-count 0.\n- MCP is genuinely healthy: nginx points at `server 127.0.0.1:8092`, local `8092/health`\n  reports store present and handshake OK, public unauth `/mcp` returns 401 as designed,\n  and the `mcp_healthy` metric is 1.0.\n- The Gerrit review gate is unaffected. `/review/health` returns\n  `{\"status\":\"ok\",\"in_flight\":0,\"queue_depth\":0,\"gerrit_auth\":\"ok\"}`,\n  `compose-review-bot-1` is `Up (healthy)`, and change 3008 received `LLM-Review+1`\n  at 12:25:14Z. The autodeploy fail-safe claim holds.\n\nSo this is exclusively a detector-selection defect. Do not widen it into a deploy or\nMCP-health change.\n\n## Fix\n\nIn `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh`:\n\n1. Live-backend resolution must prefer a RUNNING managed container that owns the current\n   nginx live port. Consult the `HostConfig.PortBindings` fallback ONLY when no running\n   managed container owns that port -- which preserves the fallback's original purpose\n   (detecting a live backend that has genuinely died) while preventing a historical\n   binding from outranking a running container.\n2. The retire sweep must be able to reap an exited stale container whose historical\n   `HostConfig` port collides with the current live port but whose identity is not the\n   current live running backend. Without this, an already-stranded container stays\n   stranded after the detector is fixed.\n\nPortability note: this is a host-side shell script driven by a systemd timer, not a\nCI-provider capability, so no CI-specific trigger is involved and the\n`project.portability` criterion is not engaged.\n\nA one-time on-box `docker rm` of the exited `rebar-mcp-c0b1ad2e8e92-8092` would clear\nthe current alarm immediately, but it is an operator action, it does not fix the defect,\nand the same state will recur on the next port ping-pong. It was deliberately NOT applied.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Live-backend resolution prefers a running managed container owning the live nginx port; the `PortBindings` fallback applies only when no running managed container owns it.\n- [ ] The retire sweep reaps an exited stale container whose historical port collides with the live port and which is not the live running backend.\n- [ ] A regression test covers the three-deploy port ping-pong (port A -> port B -> port A) and asserts no `mcp-live-backend-down` marker is emitted while a healthy running backend owns the live port.\n- [ ] A regression test asserts the detector STILL emits the marker when the live backend has genuinely died, so the fallback's real purpose is preserved.\n- [ ] `rebar-autodeploy-errors` returns to OK, verified against the `rebar/host:deploy_errors` metric after the fix reaches the box.\n- [ ] The stranded `rebar-mcp-c0b1ad2e8e92-8092` container is reaped, by the fixed sweep rather than by hand.\n","id":"011f-4424-fcfc-4ce9","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["infra","autodeploy","mcp","observability","alarm-noise","signal-quality"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"autodeploy misidentifies an exited container as the live MCP backend, firing mcp-live-backend-down forever"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789910571880213384,"uuid":"3cec09ba-b672-4c33-94ec-5126590b2025"}